{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794424308,"nonce":"41DtQqqe4upokMnTWP5xQk+I8+8=","title":"A task woken while a worker is parking it can be left parked for good","message":"`hxn-future`'s `Polled` task and its waker each write one atomic and then\nread the other. A step stores the task's id in `Wakeup::task` and then tries\n`state` SCHEDULED -\u003e POLLING; a wake moves `state` IDLE -\u003e SCHEDULED and then\nloads `task` to tell the workers whom to reschedule. The accesses were\n`Release`, `Acquire` and `AcqRel`. That is the store-buffering shape: without\nsequential consistency both reads may return the old value. The step sees\nIDLE, returns pending and the cluster parks the task; the wake sees task 0,\nwhich means \"not with a scheduler yet\", and tells nobody. The future it would\nhave settled then never settles.\n\nFound by Miri, not by a native run:\n\n    cargo miri test -p hxn-future -- async_work_builds_on_the_other_kinds\n\ndeadlocks with the default seed and with `-Zmiri-seed=1`, and passes with\nseeds 2, 3 and 4. The stuck threads are the test in `Future::wait(None)` on\nthe third future of that test (spawned on a pending future, woken straight\nafter) and the one worker in `Blocking::park`. Miri reports one available\ncore, so there is one worker.\n\nIt has not been seen natively: 30 runs of the suite on Apple Silicon and 20\non the x86 NUC before and after, and `every_wake_reaches_its_task` (4000\nwakes) passes. On x86 a failed `lock cmpxchg` still drains the store buffer,\nand on AArch64 a load-acquire is not reordered before a store-release, so\nboth happen to give the ordering the code assumed. Nothing in Rust's memory\nmodel promises it, and another target need not.\n\nRuled out on the way, by reading krio at ecf740f: the agent park protocol\n(PARKED is published, then the queues are re-checked, and `Blocking::park`\nreads the slot under the lock `unpark` takes), a wake that arrives before\nthe task is parked (`Parked::wake` records it and `park` hands the job\nback), and `spawn` and `wake` pushing to an agent's own deque (both use the\ninjector). The outbox has the same shape between `Tag::watch` and `publish`,\nbut the state it guards is read and written under the future's mutex, and\n`hxn.Inbox.watch` checks once after watching.\n\nFix: every access to `Wakeup::state` and `Wakeup::task` is `SeqCst`.\n\nAlso seen under Miri, and not defects:\n- Four tests fail because they count on what a 20 ms slice of\n  `wait_driving(None)` gets done, and Miri runs too slowly for it:\n  `a_step_may_queue_work_and_may_not_drive_it`,\n  `a_wait_without_limit_finishes_short_work_and_gives_up_on_long`,\n  `queued_work_takes_turns`, `queued_async_and_stepped_work_share_the_queue`.\n- With `-Zmiri-many-seeds` one seed in 24 ended with \"the main thread\n  terminated without waiting for all remaining threads\": the pool's workers\n  live for the process by design. `-Zmiri-ignore-leaks` is the flag for it.","files":null}]}